{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"<system-reminder>This memory is 13 days old. Memories are point-in-time observations, not live state — claims about code behavior or file:line citations may be outdated. Verify against current code before asserting as fact.</system-reminder>\n1\t---\n2\tname: project-cledger-redaction-state\n3\tdescription: \"conversation-ledger secret-redaction stack — what shipped (0.6.1/0.7.0), decisions made, and what's deferred\"\n4\tmetadata: \n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: project\n7\t  originSessionId: 48648da3-5c2c-4827-96d5-7a6ed13224df\n8\t  modified: 2026-08-03T04:10:18.432Z\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\tState of conversation-ledger's redaction work as of 2026-08-03.\n12\t\n13\t**2026-08-03 — redaction reframed as pre-push-only. MERGED to main as 0.18.0 (PR #3), after PR #2's opencode adapter landed as 0.17.0. Global `npm link` installation rebuilt; opencode plugin refreshed.**\n14\tFour fixes, all verified end-to-end against real repos/remotes, not just unit tests:\n15\t- `cledger redact` now **refuses** when the target event is already on origin, or when origin is unreachable (fails closed — an offline `ls-remote` used to look identical to a virgin remote). Gating is **per event**, not per repo, so routine pushing doesn't kill redaction for later captures. It previously warned and proceeded, which was worse than useless: `pushScopedNotes` seeds from the remote's tip and unions the local scope in, so redact-then-push **re-uploaded** the original next to the rewrite.\n16\t- `dedupById` now consults **redaction depth first**. Redacted event + original share an id by design *and* share `recorded_at` (the rewrite spreads the original), so both ordering keys tied and a canonical-JSON byte compare decided — resolving on the secret's first char vs `[` (0x5B). Every secret starting with a digit or uppercase letter won, so reads printed plaintext beside the `redaction` event claiming removal.\n17\t- Output no longer says `history squashed: yes` (severing the notes chain ≠ object purge; residue is local-only since git transfers neither reflogs nor unreachable objects).\n18\t- Malformed-but-present config is now reported instead of silently reverting all settings to default.\n19\t\n20\tKey structural insight, worth not re-deriving: **the notes ref carries its own commit chain**, so every prior note body reaches every clone and is recoverable with `git log -p refs/notes/conversation-ledger` — combined with `cat_sort_uniq` unioning *lines*, this makes true deletion impossible on this substrate. That's why redaction is now scoped to pre-push only and post-push removal is explicitly a separate destructive tool that doesn't exist. `docs/SCRATCH-redaction-does-not-redact.md` was deleted (resolved); its reflog finding was correct but local-only, and it missed the severe post-push re-upload path.\n21\t\n22\t**Shipped earlier:**\n23\t- 0.6.1 — scan/sync finding reports mask the matched span. **Superseded by 0.16.0**: masking the match wasn't enough, because the *context* around it is what tripped the rule, so reports re-seeded findings on themselves. Reports now print **coordinates only** (event id, rule, path@offset, fingerprint); readable output moved to `cledger inspect`, which writes a 0600 file outside the repo and refuses to run inside an agent session.\n24\t- 0.7.0 — opt-in `redact.knownSecrets` (default off). `cledger redact --pattern` remembers the exact scrubbed plaintext in `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json` (local, git-invisible, beside the allowlist); capture-time redaction then exact-matches those values out of every future draft under a `known-secret` rule id. Only `--pattern` feeds it (`--all` blanks whole content); sub-8-char values dropped. Enabled globally on this machine via `~/.config/cledger/config.json`.\n25\t- 0.7.1 — `keyword-assignment` scan rule tightened (value charset excludes code punctuation, killing the type-annotation / template-interpolation false positives; this repo went 93 -> 64 findings); known-secrets store written `0600` with perm repair on each write; tests now redirect HOME to a throwaway dir so the developer's global config can't change test outcomes.\n26\t\n27\t**Decisions (so the next agent doesn't re-litigate):**\n28\t- The roadmap's original \"make capture-tier a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and **rejected** — promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently rewrite innocent text, breaking the near-zero-false-positive invariant. The value-based learning above is the intended narrow form.\n29\t- Store holds plaintext by necessity (fingerprints are one-way, can't drive exact matching); acceptable because `.git/` is as local-and-unshared as the transcripts, per the transport trust boundary.\n30\t\n31\t**Deferred, roughly by urgency:**\n32\t1. **Hash the known-secrets store instead of encrypting it** — decided 2026-08-03, roadmap entry written, not built. Store `{salted sha256, byte length}` rather than plaintext: removes the secret entirely (no key to manage, nothing recoverable even by cledger), superseding the older encrypt-with-OS-keychain plan. Open design point is candidate generation, since you can't search for a value you can't reconstruct: (a) run broad scan-tier rules as candidate *generators* and confirm by hash — cheap, and their noise is harmless because the hash gate is exact, but blind to values no rule recognizes, which is exactly what the store exists for; (b) length-indexed Rabin–Karp rolling sweep — linear, subsumes (a), more code. Accepted trade (user's call): low-entropy human passwords stay dictionary-attackable; this tool targets code repos where the realistic secret is a generated token.\n33\t2. Post-push purge, re-anchor, path-based capture exclusion, auto-renormalize, harness-artifact capture, sub-turn citation anchors.\n34\t\n35\t**Dogfooding note:** this repo captures its own sessions. It once had ~64 benign scan findings (fixtures containing literal `password=supersecret123`-style strings, which no pattern can distinguish from real secrets) that held the ledger push back; as of 2026-07-26 `cledger scan` on this repo reports **no findings** — the allowlist has absorbed them, so the ledger push is no longer blocked. The open question this leaves is the roadmap's \"audit the allowlist for generalizable false-positive patterns\" item: allowlisting is per-repo, so every downstream consumer whose codebase talks *about* secrets hits the same `keyword-assignment` false positives independently.\n36\t\n37\tRelated: [[project-ledger-ecosystem]]\n38\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ft3XxH1q3QTyZJ8k9Kqm4U","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"redaction-ux","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger-worktrees/redaction-ux","head":"e9c918ccbe056afa89c6cdfa2dfe7c312545e44e","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:b8bc0200-5312-42a4-9c54-a116d8d4a925","seq":730},"id":"ev1-ba681787f7b9e9aaa5820bd501ee524b62823c8568a45cade67a19c9c0ac7bce","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-16T10:08:23.973Z","producer":{"session_id":"b8bc0200-5312-42a4-9c54-a116d8d4a925","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.233","tool":"cledger","version":"0.21.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger-worktrees/redaction-ux","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"redaction-ux","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"content":"<system-reminder>This memory is 13 days old. Memories are point-in-time observations, not live state — claims about code behavior or file:line citations may be outdated. Verify against current code before asserting as fact.</system-reminder>\n1\t---\n2\tname: project-cledger-redaction-state\n3\tdescription: \"conversation-ledger secret-redaction stack — what shipped (0.6.1/0.7.0), decisions made, and what's deferre